+## Shared execution and delivery ownership — 2026-09-05
+
+Execution owns the media-processor composition video block, analysis outbox,
+transform attempts, exact-object grant table and issuer, and dedicated source
+gateway Worker. Execution also owns publication/moderation/retry changes and
+transactional enrichment-intent creation in the video publication repository.
+Delivery owns the Stream ingest state machine, enrichment consumer, thumbnail
+serving and projection readiness gate. Delivery develops against fixtures and
+consumes the execution grant issuer once it lands; it does not duplicate the
+gateway or concurrently edit the execution-owned composition block. Shared
+wiring changes are integrated serially through execution. Delivery chooses
+its migration number only after execution migration 0120 has merged, with a
+fresh inventory check at that time.
+
+## Concrete upload lifetime recommendation — 2026-09-05
+
+The supplied reviewer recommends min(21600, 3600 + ceil(declared_bytes /
+32768)) seconds: one hour plus transfer time at 32 KiB/s, capped at six hours.
+For 500 MiB this is 19,600 seconds. The reviewer explicitly leaves owner
+ratification pending; this paragraph records a recommendation, not adoption.
+The server persists the deadline once; one-hour renewable part URLs are
+capped by remaining reservation life and cannot extend it. An expired
+reservation returns typed expiry and requires source reselection.
+
+Adoption requires verified cleanup through an incomplete-multipart lifecycle
+rule with an explicit few-day age or scheduled, fenced abort of expired issued
+and claimed reservations with no manifest. Lifecycle cleanup is eventual,
+not a six-hour storage bound. Scheduled abort must handle replay, storage
+failure and finalize races without deleting sealing work. Completed ingress
+cleanup remains a separate obligation. No deployed cleanup configuration was
+verified and no runtime lifetime changed at this recommendation checkpoint.
